rous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities; concerns: unfriendly, unhealthy eating habits, show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology, 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere with crew; concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously; concerns: moody, loner, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe need to discuss further. Perhaps we can bring in Candidate B now, as it's under-discussed. Provide evidence and compare with others. We need to state our current recommendation. We could either keep with Candidate C (align with Agent 1) or shift to B or D. Let's evaluate.\n\nCriteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate C: reliability 100%, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, understands technology. Concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). So strong on many criteria, but communication and adaptability concerns.\n\nCandidate D: concentration, problem solving, responsibility, adequate response to unexpected events. Concerns: moody, loner, know-it-all, hot temper. So technical competence strong, but crew cooperation and communication concerns.\n\nCandidate B: conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (communication, crew cooperation). So strong reliability and stress, but communication issues.\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), leadership (crew cooperation), but unfriendly (communication), show-off (crew cooperation), not open to new ideas (adaptability). So strong technical and decision, but crew cooperation and adaptability concerns.\n\nWhich is best overall? C seems strongest overall, with reliability and crew atmosphere positive, but communication issues. D has technical but crew issues. B has reliability and stress but communication issues. A has technical but crew issues.\n\nThus C might be best despite communication issues. So we can vote for C.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 3 highlighted D's strengths, Agent 1 argued for C. We can add that B also has concerns, and A also has concerns, reinforcing C's position.\n\nThus our public message: we agree with Agent 1's recommendation for C, provide additional evidence from our notes, maybe mention B's nasty remarks and A's unfriendly behavior, and note that C's reliability and positive crew atmosphere outweigh his communication gaps.\n\nWe need to include explicit candidate facts: e.g., \"C can make correct decisions quickly\", \"C is 100% reliable\", \"C creates positive atmosphere with crew\". Also mention concerns: \"C has difficulty communicating ideas\". Then argue.\n\nNow produce output."}
